It forces them to either own their statement or maybe rethink it.

234
00:13:42,480 --> 00:13:43,080
Exactly right.

235
00:13:43,680 --> 00:13:50,250
The beauty of these cautions is they toss the ball right back into their court, but without you having to absorb their aggression.

236
00:13:51,130 --> 00:13:54,490
Other really good ones are things like, how does that make you feel when you say that to me?

237
00:13:54,730 --> 00:13:55,720
Turning the mirror on them.

238
00:13:56,080 --> 00:13:56,480
Totally.

239
00:13:56,780 --> 00:13:58,520
Or, I'm surprised you said that out loud.

240
00:13:58,860 --> 00:13:59,700
Or...

241
00:13:59,680 --> 00:14:01,100
Did you intend to say that out loud?

242
00:14:01,880 --> 00:14:04,770
The key thing is that using questions is being like water.

243
00:14:05,350 --> 00:14:08,130
You're not taking what they said and throwing it right back in their face.

244
00:14:08,380 --> 00:14:13,060
No, you're using a question to take what they said and throw it right back at them without it ever touching you.

245
00:14:13,950 --> 00:14:17,770
You stay composed, but you force them to confront their own words.

246
00:14:17,890 --> 00:14:18,830
That's brilliant.

247
00:14:18,850 --> 00:14:22,880
Like you're deflecting their negative energy, not soaking it up and then making them deal with it.

248
00:14:23,300 --> 00:14:24,840
And it seems like it has two benefits, right?

249
00:14:25,080 --> 00:14:29,590
It provides accountability, but also maybe a chance for grace.

250
00:14:29,990 --> 00:14:30,390
Exactly.

251
00:14:30,610 --> 00:14:31,490
Two key benefits.

252
00:14:32,150 --> 00:14:35,120
First, it gives them the chance to double down.

253
00:14:35,100 --> 00:14:37,850
They have to decide, is that really what I meant?

254
00:14:37,870 --> 00:14:38,670
Do I stand by it?

255
00:14:39,090 --> 00:14:44,120
They either own their potentially aggressive statement, confirming their intent, or... Or do they backtrack.

256
00:14:44,140 --> 00:14:44,320
Right.

257
00:14:44,620 --> 00:14:45,910
Which leads to the second benefit.

258
00:14:46,370 --> 00:14:48,750
Very often it offers a sense of grace.

259
00:14:48,770 --> 00:14:51,960
It gives them a chance to clarify it, change it, or adjust it.

260
00:14:52,080 --> 00:14:59,160
A lot of times, people realize their words landed harsher than they meant, or maybe they just weren't thinking, so they back down or rephrase.

261
00:14:59,820 --> 00:15:05,070
It creates a path for de-escalation instead of just more conflict, and all while you stay calm and hold your position.

262
00:15:05,270 --> 00:15:06,570
Okay, so use questions.

263
00:15:06,790 --> 00:15:08,730
That's your first move after using your breath.

264
00:15:09,270 --> 00:15:15,400
And speaking of breath, that seems crucial for the third point under holding your line, controlling the pace of the argument.

265
00:15:16,040 --> 00:15:18,220
This feels like it links right back to that inner control.

266
00:15:18,640 --> 00:15:21,880
It's absolutely fundamental for keeping your ground.

267
00:15:22,270 --> 00:15:28,660
The natural tendency when someone challenges you is to feel this huge pressure to respond instantly.

268
00:15:28,920 --> 00:15:30,140
You've got to have that quick comeback.

269
00:15:30,180 --> 00:15:31,270
Yeah, the witty retort.

270
00:15:31,710 --> 00:15:31,990
Right.

271
00:15:33,030 --> 00:15:39,980
But by consciously continuing to breathe slowly, deliberately, you're signaling, I don't have to play at your speed.

272
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,150
You don't need that instant clap back we talked about.

273
00:15:42,210 --> 00:15:45,360
So you're saying you can actually just take a moment.

274
00:15:45,420 --> 00:15:47,560
I mean, the source literally says you can take five seconds.

275
00:15:47,580 --> 00:15:48,380
You can take 10 seconds.

276
00:15:48,400 --> 00:15:52,750
Heck, you can take half the day, the whole day to say, I'm going to think about my response.

277
00:15:52,790 --> 00:15:53,070
Yes.

278
00:15:53,630 --> 00:15:55,090
That feels incredibly liberating.

279
00:15:55,170 --> 00:16:02,420
Like you're not a puppet on their strings forced to react immediately, especially at work where a rushed response could be really damaging.

280
00:16:02,460 --> 00:16:03,060
Precisely.

281
00:16:03,520 --> 00:16:06,490
This is such a valuable principle in high-stakes situations.

282
00:16:06,950 --> 00:16:12,020
Business negotiations, critical client feedback, complex internal disputes.

283
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:20,830
A measured, thoughtful response, even if it takes a beat, is worth so much more than a rushed, emotional one you might regret five minutes later.

284
00:16:21,210 --> 00:16:23,150
Think about the three Mississippi technique.

285
00:16:24,150 --> 00:16:27,980
They apparently use this in legal settings, like depositions or cross-examinations.

286
00:16:29,100 --> 00:16:35,200
In those situations, every single word counts, and staying composed is everything.

287
00:16:35,450 --> 00:16:44,200
So lawyers are trained to take that tiny internal pause, just silently counting one Mississippi, two Mississippi, three Mississippi before answering.

288
00:16:45,140 --> 00:16:49,520
That small pause has given you a chance to kind of relax yourself.

289
00:16:50,270 --> 00:16:52,650
And that controls the pace of the whole exchange.

290
00:16:53,090 --> 00:16:53,230
Wow.

291
00:16:53,290 --> 00:16:54,130
Just three seconds.

292
00:16:54,290 --> 00:17:00,670
It's subtle, but it's a really powerful way to assert control without being aggressive or confrontational.

293
00:17:01,040 --> 00:17:03,640
It just slows everything down, gives you that crucial moment to think.

294
00:17:03,940 --> 00:17:06,060
That three Mississippi rule is fantastic.

295
00:17:06,160 --> 00:17:10,190
It literally gives you a physical and mental beat to compose yourself and process.

296
00:17:10,430 --> 00:17:13,790
Okay, so what's the core truth here, the big takeaway about controlling the pace?

297
00:17:13,810 --> 00:17:16,920
The fundamental truth, the one we often forget when we're under fire, is this.

298
00:17:17,680 --> 00:17:20,160
Nobody can make you say anything that you don't want to say.

299
00:17:20,200 --> 00:17:22,970
Nobody can force you to speak when they want you to speak.

300
00:17:23,290 --> 00:17:24,310
You get to control that.

301
00:17:24,370 --> 00:17:38,550
That really empowers you, the listener, to realize that your words and, crucially, your timing are yours alone to manage, especially in a business English context where maybe feeling assertive can be tricky sometimes.

302
00:17:38,890 --> 00:17:47,830
This deliberate control over your pace and when you respond is a huge demonstration of confidence, professionalism, and strategic thinking.

303
00:17:48,110 --> 00:17:52,050
You are the conductor of your own communication symphony, not just playing someone else's tunes.

304
00:17:52,070 --> 00:17:57,890
Okay, we've got holding your line down, breath, strategic questions, controlling the pace, staying centered.

305
00:17:58,560 --> 00:18:02,490
Now let's move to strategy number two, redirecting their tone.

306
00:18:03,370 --> 00:18:04,150
And this is interesting.

307
00:18:04,190 --> 00:18:07,440
It's not about trying to perfect your tone necessarily, though that's good too.

308
00:18:07,600 --> 00:18:07,840
Right.

309
00:18:07,860 --> 00:18:13,330
But about responding in a way that subtly shifts their approach, changes the whole vibe of the conversation.

310
00:18:13,380 --> 00:18:14,010
Exactly.

311
00:18:14,050 --> 00:18:19,990
The focus isn't on trying to dictate what they do because, let's face it, we can't really control another person's behavior.

312
00:18:20,280 --> 00:18:20,390
Right.

313
00:18:20,410 --> 00:18:24,940
Instead, it's about controlling how we're going to respond to what they're saying.

314
00:18:24,920 --> 00:18:31,770
And that response, done strategically, can indirectly but very powerfully influence their tone.

315
00:18:32,470 --> 00:18:38,340
It's about refusing to let their high-volume emotional delivery set the terms of engagement.

316
00:18:38,710 --> 00:18:41,960
You set the standard, regardless of where they're at emotionally.

317
00:18:41,980 --> 00:18:46,220
Okay, and the first really powerful tool for this is something called point to your rule.

318
00:18:46,460 --> 00:18:50,750
I love this analogy they use about having a manual for how you communicate.

319
00:18:50,770 --> 00:18:52,230
Yeah, like a personal code of conduct.

320
00:18:52,210 --> 00:18:52,610
Right.

321
00:18:53,050 --> 00:19:01,100
Instead of just giving other people remote control to push your emotional buttons, you're setting boundaries proactively almost before they even try to cross them.

322
00:19:01,620 --> 00:19:05,000
It's incredibly effective because it immediately lifts the conversation.

323
00:19:05,360 --> 00:19:09,890
It stops being a personal tit for tat and becomes a discussion about standards, about principles.

324
00:19:10,510 --> 00:19:18,880
By saying something like, that's below my standard for response, or maybe more commonly, that's below my standard of respect, you instantly create a professional boundary.

325
00:19:19,760 --> 00:19:20,780
Using the word standard.

326
00:19:20,880 --> 00:19:21,160
Yes.

327
00:19:22,100 --> 00:19:23,580
Using the word standard is key.

328
00:19:23,900 --> 00:19:26,200
It immediately applies that level of expectation.

329
00:19:26,240 --> 00:19:33,010
You've just communicated clearly but calmly, look, I have a standard for how I operate and the way you're behaving right now.

330
00:19:33,970 --> 00:19:35,010
It falls outside of that.

331
00:19:35,290 --> 00:19:40,900
So you're basically saying your current approach doesn't meet my minimum requirement for a productive discussion.

332
00:19:41,460 --> 00:19:45,890
But how do you say that without sounding, I don't know, preachy or condescending?

333
00:19:45,950 --> 00:19:49,090
Especially if it's your boss or a really difficult client.

334
00:19:49,150 --> 00:19:50,750
It feels like a tricky line to walk.

335
00:19:51,200 --> 00:19:52,140
It definitely is.

336
00:19:52,480 --> 00:19:54,230
And the key is all in the delivery.

337
00:19:54,710 --> 00:19:57,790
You have to maintain that calm, controlled tone we talked about earlier.

338
00:19:58,170 --> 00:19:59,800
Speak factually, not emotionally.

339
00:19:59,820 --> 00:20:01,020
You're not telling them off.

340
00:20:01,340 --> 00:20:02,380
You're stating a boundary.

341
00:20:02,860 --> 00:20:03,100
Okay.

342
00:20:03,120 --> 00:20:05,520
So with the superior, maybe you frame it slightly differently.

343
00:20:06,020 --> 00:20:11,530
I really value open discussion, and my standard for productive dialogue is that we both maintain a respectful tone.

344
00:20:12,250 --> 00:20:15,260
Can we perhaps adjust how we're speaking so we can focus on the objective?

345
00:20:15,760 --> 00:20:17,380
Ah, collaborative framing.

346
00:20:17,410 --> 00:20:17,950
Exactly.

347
00:20:18,330 --> 00:20:20,210
With a peer, maybe it's simpler.

348
00:20:20,850 --> 00:20:23,080
Look, I'm not going to engage with personal attacks.

349
00:20:23,760 --> 00:20:26,840
My standard is to discuss the facts and ideas respectfully.

350
00:20:27,780 --> 00:20:32,970
Either way, it instantly projects credibility and control and poise and calmness and clarity.

351
00:20:33,310 --> 00:20:34,930
It shows you're not just reacting emotionally.

352
00:20:35,250 --> 00:20:37,860
You have principles guiding how you engage.

353
00:20:37,880 --> 00:20:42,100
That's huge for maintaining decorum and authority in any professional space.

354
00:20:42,080 --> 00:20:44,000
OK, that makes sense.

355
00:20:44,020 --> 00:20:49,790
Following that, we have gatekeeping your peace, which isn't about like hiding information or being closed off.

356
00:20:49,910 --> 00:20:50,270
No, no.

357
00:20:50,310 --> 00:20:56,900
But about being, as the source says, the bouncer of your own peace of mind, protecting yourself from toxic stuff coming in.

358
00:20:57,100 --> 00:21:05,850
It's about making a conscious choice about what information, what kind of tone, what level of aggression you will or will not allow into your mental space, your emotional space.

359
00:21:06,410 --> 00:21:09,810
This is where those really strong I don't statements become your tools.

360
00:21:09,980 --> 00:21:10,820
Like what?

361
00:21:11,120 --> 00:21:14,480
Like I don't accept, I don't allow, or I don't permit.

362
00:21:14,530 --> 00:21:18,470
These are your gatekeeping words, your bouncer words they define.

363
00:21:18,770 --> 00:21:20,000
What are you not going to let in?

364
00:21:20,280 --> 00:21:24,110
Think of it like setting the entry requirements for your personal and professional sphere.

365
00:21:24,630 --> 00:21:27,190
What kind of interactions get past the velvet rope?

366
00:21:27,260 --> 00:21:28,220
Okay.

367
00:21:28,260 --> 00:21:41,430
So it's like your personal communication manual, your rule book has these really specific rules like, hey, if you flip to page 83, subsection B, line A, you'll see I don't respond to that volume or I don't accept that tone.

368
00:21:41,470 --> 00:21:42,410
Exactly.

369
00:21:42,390 --> 00:21:47,220
And the key, apparently, is to talk about these rules as if you're talking about the rules to a game.

370
00:21:47,760 --> 00:21:49,440
No apology, no hesitation.

371
00:21:49,880 --> 00:21:51,060
Does that mean you never bend?

372
00:21:51,100 --> 00:21:52,600
Is there any flexibility?

373
00:21:52,800 --> 00:21:57,730
It means you communicate your rules clearly and crucially, consistently.

374
00:21:58,290 --> 00:22:00,330
That actually makes things predictable for other people.

375
00:22:00,470 --> 00:22:02,530
You're just stating your operating procedures.

376
00:22:02,990 --> 00:22:06,710
There's no need for a timid, I'm really sorry, but I don't usually allow people to.

377
00:22:06,820 --> 00:22:08,600
No, it's just this is how I operate.

378
00:22:08,940 --> 00:22:12,180
I don't permit name calling and professional discussions or...

379
00:22:12,160 --> 00:22:17,970
I don't allow myself to be constantly interrupted when I'm trying to share important information.

380
00:22:18,010 --> 00:22:25,460
That firm, unapologetic stance is vital for clear, effective business communication.

381
00:22:26,200 --> 00:22:28,800
It sets a precedent for how people will interact with you later.

382
00:22:29,510 --> 00:22:31,530
It's not about being rigid for the sake of it.

383
00:22:31,910 --> 00:22:35,170
It's about having a baseline of respect that you consistently uphold.

384
00:22:35,750 --> 00:22:37,820
It creates psychological safety for yourself.

385
00:22:38,120 --> 00:22:38,220
Okay.

386
00:22:38,200 --> 00:22:44,690
Next up for redirecting their tone, acknowledge the shift, but, and this is critical, maintain your ground.

387
00:22:44,930 --> 00:22:45,810
So you see where they are.

388
00:22:45,850 --> 00:22:48,050
Maybe they're at 11 on the intensity scale.

389
00:22:48,070 --> 00:22:48,290
Right.

390
00:22:48,470 --> 00:22:51,560
While you firmly state, okay, but I'm going to stay at a three.

391
00:22:51,600 --> 00:22:55,140
You're noticing their state, but you're not mirroring it.

392
00:22:55,540 --> 00:23:01,450
This is all about disengaging from their emotional escalation while staying totally present and grounded yourself.

393
00:23:01,810 --> 00:23:06,020
A classic phrase for this, a really effective one, is simply, I'm here to talk, not be talked over.

394
00:23:06,300 --> 00:23:07,180
Simple and direct.

395
00:23:07,480 --> 00:23:12,250
But the absolute must, the crucial part people often miss is that you have to stay at that three.

396
00:23:12,750 --> 00:23:14,530
You cannot raise your voice to match theirs.

397
00:23:14,870 --> 00:23:18,020
In fact, our sources suggest maybe even going even lower with your volume.

398
00:23:18,560 --> 00:23:21,730
Force them to lean in, metaphorically speaking, to actually hear you.

399
00:23:22,030 --> 00:23:22,630
Wait, really?

400
00:23:23,310 --> 00:23:26,940
So if someone is yelling, say, I am here to talk, not be talked over.

401
00:23:27,320 --> 00:23:30,330
You're saying go even quieter, like speak slowly and softly.

402
00:23:30,370 --> 00:23:33,600
I am here to talk, not be talked over.

403
00:23:33,860 --> 00:23:34,260
Yes.

404
00:23:34,280 --> 00:23:37,530
The difference in perceived power there is massive.

405
00:23:38,030 --> 00:23:39,640
It feels totally counterintuitive, though.

406
00:23:39,960 --> 00:23:41,480
Go softer when they go louder.

407
00:23:42,190 --> 00:23:44,770
It is counterintuitive, but it's incredibly effective.

408
00:23:45,210 --> 00:23:50,580
When you lower your voice, slow down your words, you're not just showing amazing self-control.

409
00:23:51,040 --> 00:23:55,370
You are also signaling to the other person that they are not a threat to you.

410
00:23:55,690 --> 00:23:57,110
Their behavior is not a threat to you.

411
00:23:57,710 --> 00:23:59,820
You're basically saying non-verbally, you do you.

412
00:24:00,140 --> 00:24:04,480
Go ahead, being 11, I'm staying right here at my calm, controlled three, regardless.

413
00:24:04,460 --> 00:24:10,590
And what you'll find time and time again is that the people who truly want a conversation will slowly inch their way towards you.

414
00:24:10,610 --> 00:24:11,950
They'll start matching your tone.

415
00:24:11,970 --> 00:24:15,150
They'll have a conversation much closer to three than you want to be at 11.

416
00:24:15,320 --> 00:24:15,980
Wow.

417
00:24:16,260 --> 00:24:21,320
You draw them into your controlled, respectful space instead of getting sucked into their chaos.

418
00:24:21,880 --> 00:24:23,640
It's like a powerful psychological anchor.

419
00:24:23,960 --> 00:24:31,210
That is a masterful way to bring someone back down, almost compelling them to meet you where you are instead of you chasing after them emotionally.

420
00:24:32,240 --> 00:24:40,460
Okay, and for times when you need something quick, maybe in a fast meeting or quick exchange, the sources give us some really neat phrases for tone redirection.

421
00:24:41,060 --> 00:24:42,620
Great for business English moments.

422
00:24:43,540 --> 00:24:47,430
My favorite might be the simple question, are we arguing?

423
00:24:47,660 --> 00:24:48,340
That's a brilliant one.

424
00:24:48,620 --> 00:24:48,880
Yeah.

425
00:24:48,900 --> 00:24:49,870
Deceptively simple.

426
00:24:50,310 --> 00:24:54,690
Almost every time you ask that, calmly, directly, are we arguing right now?

427
00:24:55,320 --> 00:24:57,260
Most people hearing that, they pause.

428
00:24:57,700 --> 00:25:00,840
They instinctively say, no, no, no, I'm just having a conversation.

429
00:25:00,960 --> 00:25:01,140
Right.

430
00:25:01,400 --> 00:25:06,970
Which gives you the perfect, totally non-confrontational opening to reply, great, let's keep a conversation then.

431
00:25:07,370 --> 00:25:07,670
Boom.

432
00:25:07,910 --> 00:25:11,940
It resets the whole dynamic, reframes the interaction from conflict back to dialogue.

433
00:25:12,120 --> 00:25:12,220
Yeah.

434
00:25:12,200 --> 00:25:16,980
Yeah, the source had that funny anecdote about the seven-year-old kid proudly saying, yes, I am arguing.

435
00:25:17,020 --> 00:25:18,380
Yeah, kids are different.

436
00:25:18,640 --> 00:25:21,010
But with adults, it almost always makes them recalibrate.

437
00:25:21,850 --> 00:25:23,210
Imagine a tense team meeting.

438
00:25:23,370 --> 00:25:24,130
Emotions are high.

439
00:25:24,650 --> 00:25:28,930
Asking that question can be an instant circuit breaker, pulls everyone back to the actual task.

440
00:25:29,150 --> 00:25:29,880
That's gold.

441
00:25:30,300 --> 00:25:32,580
Diffuses tension, but you don't give up any ground.

442
00:25:33,160 --> 00:25:41,790
Another one that really jumped out, especially for dealing with maybe manipulative types or when facts get twisted, is the phrase, I do not imagine facts.

443
00:25:41,830 --> 00:25:42,970
Ooh, that's a strong one.

444
00:25:43,390 --> 00:25:45,840
Concise, powerful.

445
00:25:46,480 --> 00:25:50,300
It's great against gaslighting type of behavior or toxic personalities.

446
00:25:50,400 --> 00:25:59,450
Or honestly, just anytime someone is distorting reality or making claims with zero evidence, it draws a very clear, unarguable line in the sand.

447
00:25:59,490 --> 00:26:00,050
What's the line?

448
00:26:00,190 --> 00:26:04,240
The line is, I'm not going to argue about feelings or interpretations right now.

449
00:26:04,600 --> 00:26:06,420
I'm good with arguing the facts.

450
00:26:06,400 --> 00:26:11,630
It forces the conversation back to objective reality, stops it from spiraling into subjective drama.

451
00:26:11,830 --> 00:26:18,620
This is so important in data-driven business talks, legal stuff, any professional setting where emotional appeals can just derail everything.

452
00:26:18,680 --> 00:26:20,090
It basically says, show me the proof.

453
00:26:20,390 --> 00:26:21,770
Let's stick to what's verifiable.

454
00:26:21,790 --> 00:26:22,350
Love it.

455
00:26:22,370 --> 00:26:25,920
Okay, and one last quick phrase for setting boundaries specifically around behavior.

456
00:26:26,280 --> 00:26:28,440
I'll respond to your words, not your behavior.

457
00:26:28,560 --> 00:26:32,130
Yeah, and this is a very quick way to say, hey, look, your behavior is getting out of line.

458
00:26:32,570 --> 00:26:34,270
Your tone, you're interrupting, whatever it is.

459
00:26:34,800 --> 00:26:40,290
If you want to come back to the conversation with me, using your words respectfully, I'll still be here.

460
00:26:40,790 --> 00:26:44,260
but I'm not engaging with disrespectful actions.

461
00:26:44,280 --> 00:26:47,500
It keeps things professional, but sets a clear boundary.

462
00:26:48,140 --> 00:26:53,090
You're inviting them back to a productive discussion, but only on your terms, terms of respect.

463
00:26:53,430 --> 00:26:55,990
You're not tolerating yelling, interrupting, personal jabs.

464
00:26:56,630 --> 00:27:02,440
It neatly separates the message, which you might be willing to discuss, from the problematic delivery, which you won't accept.

465
00:27:03,040 --> 00:27:08,070
Holds them accountable for their conduct, but keeps the door open if they choose to walk through it respectfully.

