00:39:57,680 --> 00:39:58,780
It is, but it works.

704
00:39:58,880 --> 00:40:01,370
It establishes your credibility, your control.

705
00:40:01,750 --> 00:40:07,030
You're basically saying, I operate by this code, not by reacting emotionally.

706
00:40:07,150 --> 00:40:08,960
It shows real professionalism.

707
00:40:09,000 --> 00:40:10,420
Like you have your own internal rule book.

708
00:40:10,520 --> 00:40:11,080
Kind of.

709
00:40:11,340 --> 00:40:14,920
You become the bouncer for your own peace of mind.

710
00:40:15,220 --> 00:40:15,490
Yeah.

711
00:40:15,510 --> 00:40:19,170
Using words like, I don't accept that, or I don't permit that kind of language.

712
00:40:19,770 --> 00:40:20,650
You set the terms.

713
00:40:20,690 --> 00:40:21,950
You're not asking permission.

714
00:40:22,150 --> 00:40:22,750
Okay, I see.

715
00:40:23,260 --> 00:40:26,660
And what about when they're really yelling, like up at 11?

716
00:40:26,690 --> 00:40:28,510
You make a point of staying at your three.

717
00:40:29,090 --> 00:40:37,420
You consciously lower your voice, slow down your speech, calmly saying something like, I am here to talk, not to be talked over.

718
00:40:37,460 --> 00:40:39,430
It sends a clear signal.

719
00:40:39,670 --> 00:40:41,510
That their volume isn't intimidating you.

720
00:40:41,570 --> 00:40:41,850
Right.

721
00:40:42,030 --> 00:40:45,530
It often makes them de-escalate because they aren't getting the fight they might have been expecting.

722
00:40:45,630 --> 00:40:48,540
Other quick phrases could be, are we arguing now?

723
00:40:48,660 --> 00:40:51,280
Or I'll respond to your words, not your behavior.

724
00:40:51,450 --> 00:40:52,690
pulling it back to the substance.

725
00:40:52,830 --> 00:40:53,410
Exactly.

726
00:40:53,430 --> 00:40:54,590
Keeping it constructive.

727
00:40:54,670 --> 00:40:58,560
But sometimes, sometimes you just need to get out of there, right?

728
00:40:58,580 --> 00:40:59,460
Even with all these tools.

729
00:40:59,840 --> 00:41:00,300
Absolutely.

730
00:41:00,320 --> 00:41:01,360
And that's the third step.

731
00:41:01,930 --> 00:41:03,130
Exit with control.

732
00:41:03,170 --> 00:41:05,110
This isn't about being weak.

733
00:41:05,170 --> 00:41:07,920
It's actually a position of strength, setting a boundary.

734
00:41:07,980 --> 00:41:10,790
So first, you need to actually claim your exit.

735
00:41:10,910 --> 00:41:11,150
Yes.

736
00:41:11,730 --> 00:41:14,560
State your intention clearly, calmly.

737
00:41:14,580 --> 00:41:16,950
I need to leave this conversation now.

738
00:41:17,330 --> 00:41:18,250
And then you follow through.

739
00:41:18,270 --> 00:41:19,950
No big dramatic storm off.

740
00:41:19,970 --> 00:41:21,680
Just a clear statement and action.

741
00:41:22,040 --> 00:41:23,140
Maintains your dignity.

742
00:41:23,680 --> 00:41:25,590
What if it's becoming a real shouting match?

743
00:41:25,670 --> 00:41:26,550
Then postpone it.

744
00:41:27,110 --> 00:41:30,320
If things are getting out of control for both sides, suggest a break.

745
00:41:30,820 --> 00:41:34,440
I'm willing to come back to this later or can we pause this for an hour?

746
00:41:34,480 --> 00:41:36,300
And often they might want that break too.

747
00:41:36,500 --> 00:41:37,360
Very often, yes.

748
00:41:37,710 --> 00:41:39,890
It allows for a mutual cooling off period.

749
00:41:40,250 --> 00:41:44,500
And finally, a really strong way to exit is just declaring I'm not going there.

750
00:41:44,660 --> 00:41:45,940
When is that best used?

751
00:41:46,070 --> 00:41:52,440
Particularly useful if you feel someone is trying to bait you, maybe pull you into gossip or unproductive personal attacks.

752
00:41:52,480 --> 00:41:53,520
It's a firm boundary.

753
00:41:53,560 --> 00:41:55,340
Nope, not engaging on those terms.

754
00:41:55,500 --> 00:41:58,810
Okay, so this whole approach, it really shifts the focus.

755
00:41:59,350 --> 00:41:59,810
It does.

756
00:41:59,870 --> 00:42:04,820
Defending yourself, especially in professional settings, it stops being about winning the argument.

757
00:42:05,160 --> 00:42:09,540
It becomes about maintaining your own control, your composure, your centeredness.

758
00:42:09,860 --> 00:42:12,230
It's more like a strategic dance than a messy brawl.

759
00:42:12,410 --> 00:42:13,630
Well put.

760
00:42:13,850 --> 00:42:22,240
And these skills, they're just invaluable, not just for arguments, but think about high states meetings, negotiations, even difficult feedback sessions.

761
00:42:22,600 --> 00:42:26,200
So thinking about all this, what really stands out to you listening right now?

762
00:42:27,000 --> 00:42:31,790
Maybe consider how just one tactic, maybe taking that breath or asking, did you mean that?

763
00:42:31,990 --> 00:42:32,190
Yeah.

764
00:42:32,650 --> 00:42:34,310
Could change your next tricky conversation.

765
00:42:34,630 --> 00:42:34,910
Yeah.

766
00:42:34,970 --> 00:42:40,020
What could shift for you if you consistently practice this kind of controlled strategic communication?

767
00:42:40,060 --> 00:42:41,000
Something to think about.

768
00:42:47,950 --> 00:42:50,990
Thank you.
